<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  RKMCinemaTV — the app's real Info.plist.

  ⚠ TWO THINGS ABOUT THIS FILE, both learned the hard way on the iOS target's first real build:

  1. POINT `INFOPLIST_FILE` AT IT — Build Settings → Info.plist File = `Config/Info.plist` — and set
     GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = NO. It is a file rather than INFOPLIST_KEY_* settings for one reason:
     **App Transport Security is a nested dictionary**, which the INFOPLIST_KEY_* settings cannot
     express (apple/WORKFLOW.md §2).
  2. ⚠ **IT MUST LIVE OUTSIDE `RKMCinemaTV/`, the target's synchronized source folder.** A file inside
     that folder *is* a target member, so this plist would be at once **copied into the app as a
     resource** and **processed as the Info.plist**, and the build dies with
     `error: Multiple commands produce …RKMCinemaTV.app/Info.plist`. `Config/` is referenced only by
     the build setting and by nothing in the project — which is exactly what keeps it out of that
     automatic membership.

  ⚠ tvOS-specific keys are ABSENT on purpose. `LSRequiresIPhoneOS`, `UIApplicationSceneManifest`,
  `UILaunchScreen`, `UIUserInterfaceStyle` and `UISupportedInterfaceOrientations` are all iOS keys:
  a tvOS app has no orientations to declare, no status bar to make legible, and its interface style is
  always dark. Copying them across "for symmetry" would be four keys that do nothing but invite the
  question of which platform a setting belonged to.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>RKMCinemaTV</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<!--
	  ⚠ App Transport Security — READ BEFORE ADDING A SECOND KEY.

	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is the ONLY key here, deliberately. The address is typed by a human at
	  runtime, so it can be ANY host — a raw LAN IP, a Tailscale name, a future domain — and no
	  build-time exception list can cover that.

	  ⚠⚠ DO NOT ADD NSAllowsLocalNetworking (nor NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsForMedia /
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent) ALONGSIDE IT. On iOS 10+ / tvOS 10+ the presence of any of
	  those more specific keys makes ATS **ignore NSAllowsArbitraryLoads entirely** — so with both keys
	  set, plain http:// is blocked for every host that is not "local networking", while a LAN IP still
	  works. That asymmetry is how it presents:

	      http://rkm-hp.tail8d5e8.ts.net:8124
	      NSURLErrorDomain -1022 — the resource could not be loaded because the App Transport
	      Security policy requires the use of a secure connection

	  — a failure that reads like a server problem and is not. Paid for on the iOS shell,
	  2026-09-14: the narrower-looking key was the more damaging one.

	  ⚠ This matters MORE on tvOS than it did on iOS, for a reason that is easy to miss: ATS also
	  governs AVFoundation. The HLS playlists and segments the player will fetch in Phase C come from
	  the same private origin over the same plain http://, so omitting this declaration would not just
	  block the API — it would fail playback later with an error that points at the media, not at the
	  plist.

	  (App Review asks for a justification for NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. Irrelevant unless this is ever
	  submitted to the store — a household app with a runtime address could not pass 4.2 anyway.)
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
